<tr><td colspan=4><font size=2 face=verdana color=#ffffff>It was a bright, mid Autumn day when the cry rang out through Kelethin, announcing the birth of a beautiful blonde baby girl, who's parents jokingly named 'Quickening' due to her rapid birth.
<p>
Quickening grew up as most wood elves do, running freely through the forests of Greater Faydark, laughing, joking, and enjoying life as much as elvenly possible. She knew to avoid the cave enterance of Castle Crushbone, as that is where the dreaded Emperor Crush lived, leader of all the Orcs of Faydwer. 
<p>
Of all the children born in the same time frame as Quickening, she was the one with the least interest in nature and animals. Sure, she was appreciative of all they did and provided for her community, but her passion lay elsewhere, in music. So from a very early age, Quickening practiced singing, often sneaking out of her home high in the trees of Kelethin to listen to the bards spin tales of high adventure and daring escapes in the Heartwood Tavern. At that moment, she knew she would be like these Bright Blue and Green song weavers, and become a bard. She would provide the soundtrack for life that all nature pulses to. She would be the one telling the stories soon enough. 
<p>
Time passed slowly for Quickening, as it does for all elves. She reached an age where it was time for her to choose the path she would venture down: Her father, a ranger, and her mother, a druid hoped it would be one of the careers that invloved climbing trees and caring for sick bears. The shock and anger on their faces mixed with loss as she calmly walked into her room, retreived her mothers old leather armor, and reached under her bed...producing a crude, wooden lute that she had bought from a merchant for 3 gold pieces. She hugged both her mother and father, and exited theie home amidst sobs from her mother, and angry shouts from her father, and made her way to the bards guild, where she threw herself at the mercy of the guild leaders to teach her their craft. One of the bards that was there a grizzled old adventurer with scars on his face, smiled softly at the young blonde girl, the confidence etched on her face, and informed the leaders that if they would not teach her the ways of the bard, then he would. They said they could not, as she had yet to prove herself to them. He nodded solemnly, took the girl by the hand and led her outside, where he slowly removed his dented Green helm, and smiled down at her. "My name, dear girl, is Morgan. You will, from now, be my student."
<p>
Months passed, Quickening learning at every step, reading her teachers old notes, songs, and books on instruments and playing. He would leave on short adventures, always returning within a few days. One day, as Quickening awoke, she saw her, for lack of a better term, master, preparing himself for what looked like a war. She looked at him Inquisitively. "Firona has been kidnapped. Taken by some fould Lizardman creature. I shall return, Quick. Keep your studies constant." With that, he calmly walked over to her, ruffled her hair, and departed, two swords strapped to his back.
<p>
Nearly two months later a knock at the door was heard in the middle of the night. Quickening started to the door immediately, seeing a hulking, hideous Ogre standing there, scars running down his cheeks, one eye white from being blinded. He looked at her, and for what was an Ogre, smiled a gentle smile.
<p>
"Deer gurl...me am aqu..aqu...frehnd uv ur teechur Morgun. Me brings yous badd newz. May me come in to rel...rela..rest?"
<p>
Quick ushered this strangly gentle giant inside, as he ducked under the small door frame.
<p>
"Me name iz Crumlek...me am gr..gre..great Shaman uv da city Oggok. Me have long been advenchuring frend of Morgun, little green muzik man. Me fite with him an' uthers to free pretty lady Vie. We win, but Morgan no live. He say to me, 'Go to woodie elf city, find me cottage in da woodses, and tell blonde pretty gurl dat I hav falled, but me am going to see Tunare now.' He also wanted me's to give you dis."
<p>
The large man reached into a huge backpack that was strapped to his back, and produced a blue suit of armor, and laid it out on the bead next to Quickening.
<p>
"Dis be lamb..lambuh..."
<p>
"Lambent," Quickening sobbed through tears.'
<p>
"Ya dat it. Dis be Lambent armer. Dis good for pretty bardz like yous. Me's sorry you's lost a master Quickening...but me's more sorry dat me's lost a frend."
<p>
With that, tears rolling down his cheeks, Crumlek rose, and gently hugged the now uncontrollably crying girl, and held out his hand to her, a bag resting in it.
<p>
"Dis holdz sum foood an' watur. You need to gos and starts advenchuring toos."
<p>
With that he left, leaving a sobbing Quickening to cry herself to sleep.
<p>
You all know the rest.&nbsp;</font></td></tr>

